Both answered from the code:

Q1 — Add the platform but no conf files? Nothing breaks; it just sits idle.
Both loaders degrade gracefully (warn + empty, no crash):

No catalog.yaml → load_catalog() returns {} and logs mongo.catalog_loader.file_missing (catalog_loader.py:55-57). Any metrics collected fall back to metric_type=gauge, no unit, and the fit uses the default strategy (median_mad, k=3.5).
No/empty targets/ dir → load_targets() returns [] and logs mongo.target_loader.dir_missing (target_loader.py:59-61). Collect finds zero targets → collects nothing for that platform.
So the platform is registered and harmless — dashboards show it with 0 targets and no catalog entries, collect --platforms mongo runs clean and does nothing. (This assumes you added the code folder + registrations; if you add only the enum member without the src/platforms/mongo/ folder, the registry imports fail — but that's not "no conf files," that's missing code.)

Q2 — Yes: migrations_enabled: true, then setup.sh.
The collection/index creation is gated by a flag — conf/infra/mongo.yaml:124:


migrations_enabled: true
setup.sh runs a no-op collect whose only job is to trigger ensure_collection() — but that's wrapped in if migrations_enabled: (compute_thresholds.py:118, and the same in the collect bootstrap). So:

If it's false, setup.sh runs but skips collection + index creation → your new mongo_metrics / mongo_thresholds / etc. never get materialized (and you can't rely on first-write auto-create, because these are time-series collections + TTL + indexes that a plain insert won't set up correctly).
The comment there explains the usual pattern: steady-state often flips it off so the daemon (running under a readWrite-only account without dbAdmin) doesn't try to re-apply migrations.
So the sequence for a new platform is:

Set migrations_enabled: true.
Run bin/setup.sh under a Mongo account that has dbAdmin (to create the collections + indexes).
Optionally flip it back to false for steady-state.
Check your current value first — if your box is already in steady-state with it false, setup.sh will silently no-op on the new collections until you flip it true.

One naming heads-up: calling the monitored platform mongo means its collections are mongo_metrics, mongo_thresholds, … living in your MongoDB — not wrong, just easy to confuse with the storage layer in logs/queries. oracledb-style disambiguation (or mongodb) might read cleaner, but functionally mongo is fine.

